nodecode-windows-apps is a rewrite of Yicheng-Mark's Yicheng-Mark/hermes-windows-plugins:
https://github.com/Yicheng-Mark/hermes-windows-plugins
commit 05a01102d3c0b43a345a94ed8b0f56317692136b
MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Yicheng-Mark (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the two tools, win_app_table and win_app_detail, with their
arguments, their views, their handling of hidden SystemComponent entries, the
32/64-bit dual install collapsed to one row, and the reading of an uninstall
string into msiexec, appx, setup executable, script or other. The registry
layer is rewritten too, but not against CPython's winreg: the cell asks
reg.exe, the registry tool Windows ships, for the same four Uninstall keys and
parses what it prints, so nothing here needs Python. One thing the original's
tool reached for and this one reports in words instead: the JSON payload is
now readable text, a line of notes and a line per program, because that is
what a Nodecode tool answers with. Left out: the per-subkey "unreadable" flag,
which winreg could raise and one `reg query /s` run cannot see -- a key that
answers nothing is reported as a view that could not be read, in the notes.
